   "verdict": "partial",
   "status": "adjudicated",
   "graderA": "unfalsifiable",
   "graderB": "miss",
   "reason": "A's reading (a), generic intelligence-amplifying software, was available long before 2014 and would make the 2022 placement empty; it is not equally faithful, so unfalsifiable does not apply. B's miss overlooks consumer closed-loop neurostimulation: Dreem (2017, $499) read EEG and played phase-timed sound to deepen slow-wave sleep, and Elemind (June 2024 launch, $349, shipping Sept 2024) reads EEG and applies phase-locked acoustic stimulation for sleep onset. Both read the brain and stimulate it to accomplish a brain function, buyable by consumers within Y+2. But they target sleep, not cognition proper, and are single niche products; no cognition-augmenting closed-loop product is sold. Real but limited = partial. A strict 'cognition only' reading gives provisional miss, so this call is less certain."
  },
  {
   "id": "hz-2014-019",
   "verdict": "contested",
   "status": "contested",
   "was": "partial",
   "reason": "Graders call Eversense (2018) the single niche product, but Medtronic Reveal LINQ (FDA Feb 2014, ~1 cc implant with remote CareLink reporting) and sensing pacemakers/ICDs were sold widely before 2019, which would give hit under an implanted-biometric-sensor reading; under the poster's 'microscopic' reading nothing exists (miss). Readings diverge, partial is not supported by either."
  },
